#--: Overview

The declarations themselves live in #~"lib/system/tokenize.z", which is the one
place they are written. Every definition, field, arm, generic parameter and
own-line parameter there carries its own doc comment, and #~"zls" surfaces
each on hover; the corpus runner ratchets that coverage under #~"make ci".
This page is the prose and the worked examples -- read it for how the unit
is meant to be used, and the source for the exact surface.

The #~"tokenize" unit exposes the compiler's own lexer as a public
API -- the analogue of Python's #~"tokenize" module. It turns
zerolang source text into a stream of #~"Token" values classified
by a #~"toktype" tag. The stream is #b:"lossless": it includes the
whitespace, comment, and end-of-line trivia tokens the parser
discards, so a formatter or source-rewriter can reconstruct exact
layout.

#b:"Stability: preview." This surface ships alongside the #~"zl"
linter/formatter and may change. It is a thin re-export of the
compiler front-end (#~"lib/system/zlexer.z"); the raw
#~"zlexer.Tokenizer" carries additional internal state.

Accessed via qualified names only -- #~"tokenize.scan ...",
#~"tokenize.Tokenizer.create ...".


#--: Index

#p: #b:"Types"

#ul:
  [#*: #> to=tokenizer: #b:"Tokenizer" -- #~"class as { create: function {source: String.take fsno: zvfs.fsno} out this  ...  token: function {:this} out Token }"]
  [#*: #> to=token: #b:"Token" -- #~"class { toktype: toktype  text: zast.nameid  fsno: zvfs.fsno  lineno: u32  colno: u32  width: u32 }"]
  [#*: #> to=tt: #b:"toktype" -- #~"variant { ...51 arms: wsTok, eolTok, commentTok, eofTok, refIdTok, keywords (functionTok, ifTok, forTok, ...), literals, punctuation... }"]

#p: #b:"Functions"

#ul:
  [#*: #> to=scan: #b:"tokenize.scan" -- #~"function {source: String.take pool: StringPool} out (List Token)"]


#--: tokenizer


The raw scanner. #~"create" takes ownership of the #~"source"
string and a file number #~"fsno" (used only to stamp emitted
tokens for diagnostics -- pass the zero value #~"zvfs.fsno" for a
standalone buffer).
Each call to #~"token" returns the next #~"Token", including
#~"wsTok" / #~"commentTok" / #~"eolTok" trivia, terminated by an
#~"eofTok" token that repeats if called again.

#code language=zerolang: """
  tk: tokenize.Tokenizer.create source: src.copy fsno: zvfs.fsno
  for true loop {
      t: tk.token
      match (
          t.toktype
      ) case eofTok then {
          break
      } else null
      # ... inspect t ...
  }
"""

Most callers want #~"scan" (below) rather than driving the
tokenizer by hand.


#--: token


One lexical token. #~"toktype" is its #~"toktype" classification;
#~"text" is its spelling INTERNED in the pool the scan was given, and
#~"zast.poolTextAt" reads it back -- for a #~"wsTok" that is the run of
spaces, for a #~"commentTok" the comment text. A token carries an id
rather than its own copy because nearly every spelling in a source file
has been seen before, and the pool copies only on first sight.
#~"lineno" / #~"colno" are 1-based, and #~"width" is the token's byte
count, which is what a diagnostic underlines.


#--: tt

#~"toktype" is a #~"variant" with one arm per token kind. Trivia arms
#~"wsTok", #~"commentTok", and #~"eolTok" carry the between-token
layout; #~"eofTok" terminates the stream. The remaining arms classify
keywords, identifiers, literals, and punctuation. Match on it to
dispatch:

#code language=zerolang: """
  match (
      t.toktype
  ) case wsTok then {
      null
  } case commentTok then {
      null
  } case eofTok then {
      done = true
  } else null
"""


#--: scan


Tokenize #~"source" into its full raw token stream -- whitespace,
comments, and EOL trivia included -- terminated by the #~"eofTok"
token. The one-call convenience over #~"Tokenizer".

The pool outlives the scan, so the caller owns it and reads spellings
back from it afterwards.

#code language=zerolang: """
  pool: tokenize.StringPool.create
  toks: tokenize.scan source: src.copy :pool
  print "tokens: \{toks.length}"
"""
